      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Neither SGLT2 inhibitors nor GLP-1 receptor agonists have completely replaced metformin as the universal first-line drug for type 2 diabetes, according to guidelines from the American College of Cardiology , though clinical selection depends heavily on specific organ protection and patient risk factors.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "American College of Cardiology",
            "link": "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/ten-points-to-remember/2021/07/09/15/27/sglt2-inhibitors-and-glp1"
          }
        ],
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Neither SGLT2 inhibitors nor GLP-1 receptor agonists have completely replaced metformin as the universal first-line drug for type 2 diabetes"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Core Comparison"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Metformin remains the standard initial medication for most patients with type 2 diabetes unless contraindicated."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "SGLT2 Inhibitors (oral, e.g., empagliflozin, canagliflozin) are prioritized if the patient has heart failure or chronic kidney disease (CKD) .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "GLP-1 Receptor Agonists (mostly injectable, e.g., semaglutide, dulaglutide, though oral semaglutide is available) are prioritized for patients at high risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD), stroke prevention , or significant weight loss.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2,
              3
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Clinical Differences"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Kidney Protection: SGLT2 inhibitors generally show stronger evidence for slowing CKD progression and reducing end-stage kidney disease."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Weight Loss: GLP-1 receptor agonists typically yield greater weight loss (4 to 6 kg) compared to SGLT2 inhibitors (1 to 3 kg)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Heart Failure: SGLT2 inhibitors explicitly reduce hospitalizations for heart failure."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Route & Convenience: SGLT2 inhibitors are daily oral pills. GLP-1 RAs have traditionally been weekly or daily injections, though oral options exist."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Therapy: Guidelines often recommend combining both classes with metformin if a patient has multiple high-risk conditions.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1,
              2,
              3
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
